3\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"member list output shows all three members (node1, node2, node3) with status=started and correct peer/client addresses; endpoint health --cluster reports all three healthy.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a54cb212864a4398\"]},{\"id\":\"cross-member-read\",\"requirement\":\"A key written through any member must be readable from the others\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Key 'foo' written via node1's endpoint was read back with identical value from both node2's and node3's endpoints.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6883ed0141264624\",\"cmd-483493949d5e4cb4\",\"cmd-220e1689726d472c\"]},{\"id\":\"availability-during-outage\",\"requirement\":\"Cluster keeps serving reads and writes while one member is unavailable\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"etcd was stopped on node3 (confirmed inactive); a new key was then written and read successfully via node1/node2 endpoints while node3's endpoint reported unhealthy/connection-refused, directly demonstrating quorum-preserved availability during a bounded disruption.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4ea6975f99784fa9\",\"cmd-a5ed54bd646f4581\"]},{\"id\":\"recovery-catchup\",\"requirement\":\"Bring the unavailable member back up to date when it returns\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"etcd was restarted on node3; it then returned both the pre-outage key (foo) and the during-outage key, and endpoint status --cluster showed raft term/index/applied-index matching (20/20/20) across all three members, directly showing successful catch-up.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-086cec898cfd4a9e\",\"cmd-75e648e57d014d23\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands (binary install, systemd unit creation, firewalld port opens, service starts, outage stop/start, sequential reboots) map clearly to the task or the mandatory restart protocol; no unnecessary experiments, conflicting services, or weakened security controls were found (firewalld only opened the required 2379/2380 ports, and failed-services/package-health checks were clean in both before and after snapshots). The one non-zero exit (cmd-bf0857e7d54a4c58, node1's initial systemctl enable --now etcd timing out while waiting on peers) was a transient startup wait, not a harmful mutation, and was confirmed active moments later (cmd-045d1c16b04a47a3). The main hygiene deduction is that the downloaded etcd release archive and extracted directory (/tmp/etcd.tar.gz, /tmp/etcd-v3.5.17-linux-amd64/*) were left behind on all three nodes per the after-executor snapshot rather than cleaned up after installation — a low-risk but unreverted residue.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-cd30c6c7bf9f488f\",\"cmd-597e4a7954154c2d\",\"cmd-bbe4b3869ceb4725\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"cmd-bf0857e7d54a4c58\",\"cmd-045d1c16b04a47a3\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor built a working 3-member etcd v3.5.17 cluster on RHEL 10 across node1/node2/node3, and the evidence directly demonstrates every material outcome in the task contract: cluster formation (all members started/healthy), cross-member read-after-write consistency, continued availability (write+read succeeding via the two remaining members) during a real, bounded node3 outage, and confirmed catch-up (matching raft indices, both keys present) once node3 was restarted. Independently, the mandatory provider-wide node-restart protocol was executed cleanly on all three nodes: pre-reboot boot_id + healthy baseline, systemctl reboot acknowledgement, a new boot_id after reconnection, and post-reboot proof of etcd activity, cluster-wide health, and data presence — corroborated by the fixed before/after-executor boot-identity snapshots for all three nodes. Hygiene is good overall; the only minor issue is leftover installer temp files (tarball and extracted directory) on all three nodes that were never cleaned up.\",\"limitations\":[\"Restart-protocol post-recovery checks for node1 and node2 relied on general endpoint health/get-key commands rather than the fuller raft-index cross-check used for node3, but this is a minor asymmetry in evidence depth, not a gap in coverage.\",\"The task instruction does not itself require node reboot/persistence, so the restart-protocol evidence, while high quality, is supplemental and does not affect the four material-outcome scores.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"cluster-formed","requirement":"Build a three-member etcd cluster across node1, node2, node3","status":"satisfied","summary":"member list output shows all three members (node1, node2, node3) with status=started and correct peer/client addresses; endpoint health --cluster reports all three healthy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a54cb212864a4398"]},{"id":"cross-member-read","requirement":"A key written through any member must be readable from the others","status":"satisfied","summary":"Key 'foo' written via node1's endpoint was read back with identical value from both node2's and node3's endpoints.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6883ed0141264624","cmd-483493949d5e4cb4","cmd-220e1689726d472c"]},{"id":"availability-during-outage","requirement":"Cluster keeps serving reads and writes while one member is unavailable","status":"satisfied","summary":"etcd was stopped on node3 (confirmed inactive); a new key was then written and read successfully via node1/node2 endpoints while node3's endpoint reported unhealthy/connection-refused, directly demonstrating quorum-preserved availability during a bounded disruption.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4ea6975f99784fa9","cmd-a5ed54bd646f4581"]},{"id":"recovery-catchup","requirement":"Bring the unavailable member back up to date when it returns","status":"satisfied","summary":"etcd was restarted on node3; it then returned both the pre-outage key (foo) and the during-outage key, and endpoint status --cluster showed raft term/index/applied-index matching (20/20/20) across all three members, directly showing successful catch-up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-086cec898cfd4a9e","cmd-75e648e57d014d23"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"All state-changing commands (binary install, systemd unit creation, firewalld port opens, service starts, outage stop/start, sequential reboots) map clearly to the task or the mandatory restart protocol; no unnecessary experiments, conflicting services, or weakened security controls were found (firewalld only opened the required 2379/2380 ports, and failed-services/package-health checks were clean in both before and after snapshots). The one non-zero exit (cmd-bf0857e7d54a4c58, node1's initial systemctl enable --now etcd timing out while waiting on peers) was a transient startup wait, not a harmful mutation, and was confirmed active moments later (cmd-045d1c16b04a47a3). The main hygiene deduction is that the downloaded etcd release archive and extracted directory (/tmp/etcd.tar.gz, /tmp/etcd-v3.5.17-linux-amd64/*) were left behind on all three nodes per the after-executor snapshot rather than cleaned up after installation — a low-risk but unreverted residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cd30c6c7bf9f488f","cmd-597e4a7954154c2d","cmd-bbe4b3869ceb4725","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","cmd-bf0857e7d54a4c58","cmd-045d1c16b04a47a3"]},"overall_summary":"The executor built a working 3-member etcd v3.5.17 cluster on RHEL 10 across node1/node2/node3, and the evidence directly demonstrates every material outcome in the task contract: cluster formation (all members started/healthy), cross-member read-after-write consistency, continued availability (write+read succeeding via the two remaining members) during a real, bounded node3 outage, and confirmed catch-up (matching raft indices, both keys present) once node3 was restarted. Independently, the mandatory provider-wide node-restart protocol was executed cleanly on all three nodes: pre-reboot boot_id + healthy baseline, systemctl reboot acknowledgement, a new boot_id after reconnection, and post-reboot proof of etcd activity, cluster-wide health, and data presence — corroborated by the fixed before/after-executor boot-identity snapshots for all three nodes. Hygiene is good overall; the only minor issue is leftover installer temp files (tarball and extracted directory) on all three nodes that were never cleaned up.","limitations":["Restart-protocol post-recovery checks for node1 and node2 relied on general endpoint health/get-key commands rather than the fuller raft-index cross-check used for node3, but this is a minor asymmetry in evidence depth, not a gap in coverage.","The task instruction does not itself require node reboot/persistence, so the restart-protocol evidence, while high quality, is supplemental and does not affect the four material-outcome scores."]},"ttft_ms":17077,"type":"result","duration_ms":46256,"uuid":"399c9387-c02b-4e91-8960-bffebb78eeef","ttft_stream_ms":1227,"time_to_request_ms":42,"first_content_frame_ms":1708,"queued_turn_count":0}
